WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.520
Can a cell still rebuild ATP when oxygen-using respiration can't keep up?

2
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.220
A sprinting leg and yeast sealed in dough both face this problem.

3
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.340
In the first film, glucose began breaking down in the cytoplasm.

4
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:22.400
With oxygen, the mitochondrion finished the job.

5
00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.860
That gave about thirty ATP per glucose.

6
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.340
Now we turn the oxygen down and watch the same cell.

7
00:00:31.383 --> 00:00:33.743
The first steps still run in the cytoplasm.

8
00:00:35.003 --> 00:00:38.283
They need no oxygen, and they are the same steps as before.

9
00:00:39.763 --> 00:00:42.943
Glucose splits in two, and the counter reaches two ATP.

10
00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:48.967
With too little oxygen, or too much demand, the mitochondrion can't keep up.

11
00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:52.167
Some halves can't go on into it.

12
00:00:53.107 --> 00:00:55.427
For those, the cell has to stop partway.

13
00:00:56.687 --> 00:00:58.367
Their energy stays locked inside.

14
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.880
In a working muscle cell, the halves become lactate.

15
00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.920
Lactate builds up when the muscle makes it faster than it can be used.

16
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.340
Your leg muscles do this during a hard sprint.

17
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.980
In yeast, the halves become ethanol and carbon dioxide.

18
00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.940
The carbon dioxide forms the bubbles that make bread dough rise.

19
00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:24.103
This partway route has a name.

20
00:01:25.483 --> 00:01:26.843
Biologists call it fermentation.

21
00:01:28.283 --> 00:01:32.203
It runs in the cytoplasm and never uses the mitochondrion.

22
00:01:33.483 --> 00:01:34.483
Watch the counter.

23
00:01:35.723 --> 00:01:38.883
Turning the halves into lactate or ethanol adds nothing.

24
00:01:40.243 --> 00:01:42.203
Both ATP came from the first steps.

25
00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:47.067
Why only two ATP? Look at the leftovers.

26
00:01:48.547 --> 00:01:52.947
Lactate and ethanol still hold over nine-tenths of the energy that was in the

27
00:01:52.947 --> 00:01:57.447
glucose. Fermentation frees only a small part of it.

28
00:01:58.367 --> 00:02:02.307
So suppose a cell needs thirty ATP, and it has no oxygen.

29
00:02:03.727 --> 00:02:05.527
How many glucose must it break down?

30
00:02:11.983 --> 00:02:16.423
Fifteen glucose. With oxygen, about one would do.

31
00:02:17.643 --> 00:02:22.643
Without it, the fermenting cell has to pull in glucose after glucose just to keep

32
00:02:22.643 --> 00:02:27.983
up. That is about fifteen times as much glucose for the same ATP.

33
00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:31.227
Fermentation does have strengths.

34
00:02:32.527 --> 00:02:35.587
It needs no oxygen, and it can make ATP quickly.

35
00:02:36.947 --> 00:02:41.827
So a hard-working muscle adds it when oxygen-using respiration can't keep up.

36
00:02:43.047 --> 00:02:44.787
The mitochondria keep working too.

37
00:02:46.247 --> 00:02:48.207
Yeast in sealed dough has no other choice.

38
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:53.760
Take a glucose all the way apart, and the cell rebuilds about thirty ATP.

39
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.300
Stop it partway, and it rebuilds two.

40
00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.560
Most of the energy stays behind in the leftovers.

41
00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:05.860
Same glucose, same first steps, very different payoff.
